## 11. Make the landing page configurable

ARTIC Super Admin should be able to manage public content without requiring a code change for every update. This includes:

- Homepage headlines, descriptions, images and calls to action.
- Enabled marketplace categories and business model templates.
- Featured companies, services and vehicles.
- Country, language and currency options.
- Public company listing and verification display policies.
- FAQs, announcements, support contacts and legal-document versions.
- SEO titles, descriptions, social sharing images and indexing rules.
- Homepage section ordering and visibility.

Company administrators should manage their own public profiles, service listings, locations, images and pricing, but should not be able to edit ARTIC's global identity, other companies' listings or platform-wide legal and security policies.

## 12. Search engine visibility and performance

Build the public pages so people can find relevant transport and fleet services through search engines. Use descriptive page titles, unique descriptions, clean URLs, structured metadata where appropriate, sitemap generation, canonical URLs and controlled indexing.

Only publicly approved listings should be indexed. Private owner details, customer information, contracts, account pages, internal dashboards, unpublished listings and private documents must not leak through search results, page source, APIs or file URLs.

The landing page should load quickly on mobile connections, support modern browsers and remain usable on lower-powered devices. Use server-rendered or statically generated public content where appropriate, with private data fetched only through authorized application routes.

## 13. Acceptance criteria before launch

The landing page and its account flows should not be considered complete until these checks pass:

- Every visitor category has a clear path to the right next step.
- An existing user can accept an invitation without creating a duplicate account.
- A single user can hold multiple approved roles and company memberships.
- Self-selected roles never grant privileged permissions without required approval.
- Company registration and company approval are separate, auditable states.
- Marketplace listings display only information authorized for public viewing.
- Public prices and availability come from actual configured records.
- Sign-in, invitation, email verification and password recovery are secure.
- Company A cannot access Company B's private information by changing a URL or API identifier.
- The website works on mobile, keyboard navigation and accessible display settings.
- Contact, privacy, terms and support information are present and accurate.
- All advertised features, verification badges and trust claims reflect real capabilities.

Final recommendation: Build ARTIC as a public marketplace connected to one shared identity and a modular, permission-controlled business application. The landing page should be the entry point to the whole ecosystem—not a separate website that creates disconnected accounts, duplicated vehicles or isolated booking and payment records.

 

 

 

 

 

